%[text] %[text:anchor:T_DEF03274] # Calculus: Derivatives
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_E8C62B23] ## Scripts
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_727F8117] ### [The Definition of the Derivative](file:./Scripts/Definition.m)
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,537],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | **`Definition.m`** | **In this script, students will...** |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | ![An animation showing the secant line between a fixed point on a curve and a moving point on the curve.](text:image:74aa) | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain the limit definition of the derivative and its relationship to secant lines.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} identify the sign of the derivative at a point as positive, negative, or zero based on the graph of a function.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} identify points where the derivative of a continuous function does not exist and explain why with reference to the definition of the derivative.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} recognize $f'(x)${"altText":"f'(x)"}, $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}${"altText":"(df)\/(dx)"}, and $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left\[f(x)\\right\]${"altText":"(d)\/(dx)\[f(x)\]"} as equivalent notation for the derivative of $f${"altText":"f"} with respect to $x${"altText":"x"}.</p> |
%[text:table]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_77AFFCCB] ### [Derivatives of Polynomials](file:./Scripts/PowerRules.m)
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,538],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | **`PowerRules.m`** | **In this script, students will...** |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | ![The difference quotient calculation showing that the difference quotient of ((x+h)^6-x^6)/h = 6x^5 + O(h)](text:image:7c8d) | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain why the limit definition of the derivative results in the power rule for derivatives.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} compute the derivatives of linear combinations of powers by hand.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} apply derivatives to solve practical questions.</p> |
%[text:table]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_9F419170] ### [Derivatives of Transcendental Functions](file:./Scripts/TranscendentalsRules.m)
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,523],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | **`TranscendentalsRules.m`** | **In this script, students will...** |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | ![Approximating the derivative of y=sin(theta) with step size h=0.2 plotting a sine curve and cosine curve, respectively](text:image:5e3f) | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} determine the functional relationship of the derivatives of sines, cosines, exponentials, and natural logarithms.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain the pattern in the derivatives of $\\sin(ax)${"altText":"sin(ax)"}, $\\cos(ax)${"altText":"cos(ax)"}, and $\\exp(ax)${"altText":"exp(ax)"} for constant values of $a${"altText":"a"} and that the derivative of $\\ln(ax)${"altText":"ln(ax)"} does not fit the simple pattern.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} apply the correct units to derivatives of any order.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explore simple first- and second-order differential equations that describe a physical system and use them to create mathematical models of the system.</p> |
%[text:table]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_DBF5C0DF] ### [Chain Rule and Product Rule](file:./Scripts/CombinationRules.m)
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,541],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | **`CombinationRules.m`** | **In this script, students will...** |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | ![A blue rectangle f(x) wide and g(x) tall bordered by green rectangles Δf by g and Δg by f; in the top right corner a red rectangle measures Δf by Δg ](text:image:637c) | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain why the chain rule is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left\[f(g(x))\\right\] = f'(g(x))\*g'(x)${"altText":"(d)\/(dx)\[f(g(x))\] = f'(g(x))\*g'(x)"}.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} apply the chain rule to calculate derivatives.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} explain why the product rule is $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left\[f(x)\\cdot g(x)\\right\] = f(x)\\cdot\\frac{\\text{d}g}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot g(x)${"altText":"(d)\/(dx)\[f(x) \* g(x)\] = f(x) \* (dg)\/(dx)+(df)\/(dx) \* g(x)"}.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} apply the product rule to calculate derivatives.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} practice fluently recognizing and applying the rules for computing derivatives.</p> |
%[text:table]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_98D870A0] ### [Taylor Polynomials](file:./Scripts/TaylorPolynomials.m)
%[text:table]{"columnWidths":[-1,541],"ignoreHeader":true}
%[text] | **`TaylorPolynomials.m`** | **In this script, students will...** |
%[text] | --- | --- |
%[text] | ![An animation of a Taylor polynomial of increasing degree becoming increasingly better fits to a multi-peaked function](text:image:67cc) | <p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} compute tangent lines.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} compute quadratic approximations.</p><p>$\\bullet${"altText":"bullet"} compute higher order polynomial approximations.</p> |
%[text:table]
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_A8913F44] ## App: [Calculus Flashcards](<matlab: run("CalculusFlashcards.mlapp")>)
%[text] Practice recognizing and solving standard computational problems in calculus.
